  "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- First, we plot the graph of $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/e422248a8c0b44250355c897b7825baedcdd7285a13f4547180e4f21e14e7d90.png)\n\n- To plot $y={5}^{x+3},$ we shift the graph of $y={5}^{x}$ horizontally by $3$ units to the left. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(-3,1),$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/2ed40db9640d07155f5d75f37e20f52005ef7de97028089c5335ae5445b72499.png)\n\n- To plot $y={5}^{x+3}+4,$ we shift the graph of $y={5}^{x+3}$ vertically by $4$ units up. The point $(-3,1)$ moves to $(-3,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/29e8fe8c17324bff60e076f268f771896ac990a2c559348b985aef2af48a7467.png)",
